    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a lineup anchored by top-of-the-order dynamism against left-handed pitching. **Trea Turner** (59.46) remains our highest-scoring start, benefiting from a +10% order boost and a favorable platoon setup against Manaea. He is closely shadowed by **Carson Benge** (59.21), who also draws a +10% leadoff premium; despite facing a tougher arm in Nola, the 7% platoon advantage keeps him firmly in the elite-start tier. Meanwhile, **Caleb Durbin** (58.51) and **Kyle Schwarber** (57.62) serve as our most reliable mid-tier producers, with **Kyle Schwarber** specifically bolstered by an elite 0.388 xwOBA that helps mitigate the skill of the opposing starter. **Nelson Velazquez** also warrants attention; despite a moderate score of 54.86, his 0.440 xwOBA makes him a high-upside play if he capitalizes on his position in the cleanup spot.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved balancing our marginal starters against a lack of high-ceiling alternatives. **Kazuma Okamoto** (47.99) ultimately grabbed the UTIL spot over other bench options, though his score was dragged down by an unfavorable right-on-right matchup against Gray. Our entire bench\u2014including stars like **Yordan Alvarez** and **Junior Caminero**\u2014is effectively neutralized today due to team off-days, rendering the \"closest to the cut\" analysis moot for this specific rotation. We are operating with a lean roster today, and with **Hyeseong Kim** officially in the minors, our depth remains restricted. Managers should monitor **Gary Sanchez** (46.26) closely; his score is tethered to a significant -10% penalty for the opposing starter\u2019s skill, making him our lowest-projected starter. Ensure all lineups are finalized before first pitch, as any further negative shifts in park factors or late-breaking lineup changes could drop these marginal plays below our threshold for game cap preservation.",
